---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import ProjectFacts from "../../../components/projects/ProjectFacts.astro";
import ProjectFact from "../../../components/projects/ProjectFact.astro";

// Plain ASCII on purpose: box-drawing glyphs aren't in the font's latin
// subset and would fall back to another font, breaking the alignment.
const diagram = ` me + my people              me
        |                     |
  +-----v-----+         +-----v-----+
  |  telegram |         |  discord  |
  |  the work |         | the fixes |
  +-----+-----+         +-----+-----+
        |                     |
        +----------+----------+
                   |
      +------------v------------+
      |           tiku          |
      |  built on hermes agent  |
      |  remembers with honcho  |
      +-------------------------+
      |   an lxc container on   |
      |  proxmox, in my homelab |
      +-------------------------+`;
---

<ProjectLayout project="tiku">
	<section class="intro">
		<p>
			Tiku is my personal AI assistant. I built it for myself and a handful of
			close friends and family, to take care of the small stuff that's easy to
			drop: the email that needed a reply last week, the task I said I'd do
			"tomorrow", and the odd bit of work I'd rather hand off.
		</p>
		<p>
			It isn't a product, and it isn't open to the public. If you're reading
			this, you probably got the link from me. 👋
		</p>
	</section>

	<ProjectFacts>
		<ProjectFact label="built on">
			<a href="https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/" data-umami-event="tiku-hermes">Hermes Agent</a>, by Nous Research
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="memory">
			<a href="https://honcho.dev/" data-umami-event="tiku-honcho">Honcho</a>, by Plastic Labs
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="runs on">
			an LXC container on Proxmox, in my <a href="/blog/my-proxmox-homelab-kept-freezing-every-few-hours" data-umami-event="tiku-homelab">homelab</a>
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="talks on">Telegram & Discord</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="made for">me, friends & family</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="status">live, invite-only</ProjectFact>
	</ProjectFacts>

	<h2>How it's set up</h2>

	<p>
		Tiku is built on <a href="https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/">Hermes Agent</a>,
		the open-source agent from Nous Research. Hermes does the heavy lifting
		(tools, skills, scheduled jobs, and the gateway that connects it to chat
		apps), and Tiku is my own setup of it.
	</p>
	<p>
		For memory, it uses <a href="https://honcho.dev/">Honcho</a>. Instead of
		starting from scratch every time, Tiku remembers context across
		conversations and slowly builds an understanding of each person it talks
		to: what they're working on, and how they like things done.
	</p>
	<p>
		All of it runs in an LXC container on the Proxmox server in my homelab,
		on hardware that sits at home with me.
	</p>

	<pre
		class="diagram"
		role="img"
		aria-label="Telegram and Discord both connect to Tiku, which is built on Hermes Agent, uses Honcho for memory, and runs in an LXC container on Proxmox in a homelab."
	>{diagram}</pre>

	<h2>Where it lives</h2>

	<p>
		<strong>Telegram is where the real work happens.</strong> That's where Tiku
		nudges me about emails that need attention, reminds me of tasks I've
		forgotten about, and picks up whatever I throw at it on demand.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Discord is the workshop.</strong> It's where Tiku's development
		happens: bugs, rough edges, and anything I need to fix on the bot.
	</p>

	<h2>What it does</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Email nudges.</strong> Keeps an eye on the inbox and pings me
			when something needs a reply, before it goes stale.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Forgotten-task nudges.</strong> Brings back the things I meant
			to do before they slip through the cracks.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>On-demand work.</strong> Whatever I ask for in the moment, from
			looking something up to drafting a message.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Using Tiku</h2>

	<p>
		Tiku is invite-only, for friends and family. If that's you and you'd like
		access, just ask me.
	</p>
	<p>
		Using Tiku means you agree to its
		<a href="/projects/tiku/terms-of-service/">terms of service</a> and
		<a href="/projects/tiku/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a>. I've written
		both to be readable: they cover what Tiku does with your messages, who
		else is involved, and how to get your data deleted. Questions? Write to
		<a href="mailto:hello@harshitbudhraja.com">hello@harshitbudhraja.com</a>.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
	.intro {
		margin: 2rem 0;
	}

	p,
	li {
		font-size:   1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}

	li + li {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	h2 {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
	}

	/* Scales down on small screens so all 37 columns fit, no scrolling. */
	.diagram {
		margin:           1.5rem 0;
		padding:          1rem 0.75rem;
		background-color: var(--code-bg);
		border-radius:    4px;
		font-size:        min(0.8rem, 2.9vw);
		line-height:      1.35;
	}
</style>
